---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we protect your files"
  description={`How ${site.name} keeps your files on your device, what we load from the internet, and how to check for yourself that nothing is uploaded.`}
  canonicalPath="/how-we-protect-your-files"
>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page__intro">
        <p class="micro">Trust</p>
        <h1>How we protect your files</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          “Processed on your device” is easy to say. This page explains what it
          actually means, what we do load, and how you can verify it in about a
          minute.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your files are opened and processed inside your browser.</li>
          <li>There is no upload step, because there is no server for files.</li>
          <li>You can check this yourself in your browser's Network tab. Offline support is coming.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What “processed on your device” means</h2>
        <p>
          A website can ask your browser to read a file and change it without
          sending it anywhere. This is how the tools are being built. The page is
          the program: when it loads, your browser downloads the tool code.
          After that, the code reads the file directly from your device, does
          the work, and gives you a new file to save.
        </p>
        <p>
          Think of it like a calculator on a web page. The page loads once, then
          the maths happens on your computer. Your numbers are not sent away.
        </p>

        <h2>Check it yourself</h2>
        <p>
          The tools are still being built, so these checks apply once a tool is
          live. Each tool page says whether it is ready.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Open a tool page and load your file. Let the tool finish once.
          </li>
          <li>
            Turn off Wi-Fi and mobile data. Try the same job again. If it still
            works, the processing is local. (This is a quick check, not offline
            support. Real offline support is coming.)
          </li>
          <li>
            On a computer, open the browser's developer tools (F12 or
            right-click → Inspect), go to the Network tab, and watch the
            requests while you use a tool. You will see page files and code
            being downloaded, but not your document being sent out.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What we load from the internet</h2>
        <p>
          The page itself, styles, images and tool code are downloaded from the
          site's hosting. Some tools use open-source libraries, which may be
          loaded from a content delivery network (CDN) such as jsDelivr or
          unpkg. That is a download of code, not an upload of your files.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Video to MP3 tool downloads a conversion engine (around 30 MB of
          code) from cdn.jsdelivr.net, a public third-party CDN. It only starts
          after you choose a file and press Convert. This is the only outside
          request the tool makes. It is code coming in. Your video is never
          sent. Like any CDN, jsDelivr can see your IP address and that you
          asked for those code files. We use a CDN because the engine is too
          large for our hosting's per-file limit. In your browser's Network
          tab you will see only downloads of those two code files.
        </p>

        <h2>What we never do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We never send your files to a server for processing.</li>
          <li>We never store your files.</li>
          <li>We never ask for an account or an email to use a tool.</li>
          <li>We never sell personal information.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Ads and analytics</h2>
        <p>
          The site does not show ads at the moment. If ads are added, they will
          be normal banners or native ads, clearly labelled, and never placed
          between you and a download button. Advertising partners may use
          cookies, which is why a consent banner will appear where the law
          requires one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Analytics, if enabled, is aggregate and privacy-friendly. It does not
          receive the contents of your files. The{" "}
          <a href="/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> has the details.
        </p>

        <h2>Still not sure?</h2>
        <p>
          That is a healthy attitude. Use the checks above, or open the
          developer tools and watch the network requests yourself. If something
          looks wrong, please <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
